WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.200
The Bugs Bunny Crazy Castle!

00:00:02.420 --> 00:00:08.080
Our hero, Bugs Bunny, needs your help on a daring quest to rescue his main squeeze, Honey Bunny!

00:00:08.920 --> 00:00:25.940
Nostalgia! And welcome to NEStalgia!

00:00:26.420 --> 00:00:30.120
A chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:30.340 --> 00:00:31.760
I'm Mike. I'm Sean.

00:00:32.240 --> 00:00:33.140
And I'm Joe.

00:00:33.140 --> 00:00:41.920
And I know I just made this comment last week, but we have to talk about this all over again for people who don't listen to the end of the episodes, because I just, I can't figure it out.

00:00:42.260 --> 00:00:47.880
It's obviously some kind of translation error, but the Bugs Bunny Crazy Castle.

00:00:48.140 --> 00:00:49.180
Well, who, what is that for?

00:00:49.280 --> 00:00:50.700
What is the the there for?

00:00:50.880 --> 00:00:53.180
It's, it's not Bugs Bunny's Crazy Castle.

00:00:53.240 --> 00:00:55.160
He doesn't have any ownership over the castle.

00:00:55.420 --> 00:01:00.360
It's not like Bugs Bunny, you know, is in a crazy castle.

00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:03.380
It's the Bugs Bunny crazy castle.

00:01:03.620 --> 00:01:06.040
So the whole thing is like one entity.

00:01:07.020 --> 00:01:18.600
Yeah, I mean, it's sort of like, uh, um, the, the Hollywood Hilton, you know, it's, it's specifically the crazy castle for Bugs Bunny, but it's not his.

00:01:19.040 --> 00:01:22.320
Yeah, it could also just be so we don't get him confused with any other Bugs Bunnies.

00:01:22.400 --> 00:01:24.060
Like, no, this is, this is the one you're thinking of.

00:01:24.140 --> 00:01:25.320
This is the Bugs Bunny.

00:01:25.320 --> 00:01:26.740
Right. I did think that one.

00:01:26.900 --> 00:01:34.300
I thought that one too, Joe, but there's just something about, uh, like not having like any, any emphasis or, or anything.

00:01:34.460 --> 00:01:38.600
Just, just having the almost like, it's almost like they could have taken it off the box.

00:01:38.700 --> 00:01:40.020
Cause it's, it's in the upper corner.

00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:43.240
It's not even like big splashing text with the rest of it.

00:01:43.240 --> 00:01:46.220
So they didn't even need it, but they decided to throw it on anyway.

00:01:46.420 --> 00:01:48.040
Maybe as a last minute decision.

00:01:48.460 --> 00:01:49.980
Hmm. Could be, could be.

00:01:50.100 --> 00:01:56.340
Yeah. They were like, well, people aren't going to get, people aren't going to get it without the, the, the, Hey, here's an idea.

00:01:56.740 --> 00:01:59.920
Drop the, the, wow.

00:01:59.960 --> 00:02:03.180
I mean, you know, if Facebook knew how to hit Bugs Bunny.

00:02:04.780 --> 00:02:11.360
Anyway, uh, we're talking about the Bugs Bunny crazy castle in which you play as the Bugs Bunny and you have to rescue Honey Bunny.

00:02:11.640 --> 00:02:13.040
I've never heard of this character before.

00:02:13.240 --> 00:02:19.940
If you look at the back of the box, it's just like Bugs Bunny in a dress, which is like something he does in many episodes.

00:02:19.940 --> 00:02:22.180
He always like dresses and drag and stuff like that.

00:02:22.220 --> 00:02:27.180
It's also the only name, uh, described in the manual that doesn't have a trademark next to it.

00:02:27.320 --> 00:02:29.180
So I think it was just made up for this.

00:02:29.180 --> 00:02:33.980
Yes. Well, no, so I looked it up and it looks like she appeared in comics in the sixties.

00:02:34.480 --> 00:02:36.140
Oh, just according to the wiki.

00:02:36.400 --> 00:02:38.100
But I thought the same thing when I first saw it too.

00:02:38.220 --> 00:02:49.640
I was like, are we, is it such a video game trope of like the hero saving, like the princess slash girlfriend that like they needed to like dig up this comic book character from the sixties and be like, Oh yeah, of course.

00:02:49.780 --> 00:02:56.460
That's the thing that Bugs Bunny never does, which is like, go rescue the princess is what he has to do because of the video game.

00:02:57.620 --> 00:03:01.200
So like, was, like, was Lola not a thing yet or did that?

00:03:01.240 --> 00:03:02.540
No, Lola's for space jam.

00:03:02.720 --> 00:03:04.820
It was exclusive to space jam.

00:03:05.960 --> 00:03:07.660
And which version of Lola too?

00:03:07.860 --> 00:03:09.560
I mean, you know, there's two different Lolas now.

00:03:09.660 --> 00:03:13.120
There's Lola Bunny, the, uh, the space jam character.

00:03:13.420 --> 00:03:19.720
And then there's Lola Bunny, the, the Looney Tunes show character, which is a fantastic show on Cartoon Network.

00:03:19.900 --> 00:03:21.360
I think it's streaming everywhere on HBO max.

00:03:21.480 --> 00:03:24.580
If you haven't watched it, you'll love old Looney Tunes.

00:03:24.580 --> 00:03:25.540
And show it's fantastic.

00:03:25.800 --> 00:03:28.080
All that to say that I'm a huge Looney Tunes fan.

00:03:28.280 --> 00:03:29.240
I love Looney Tunes.

00:03:29.340 --> 00:03:33.980
So I'm, I was excited to, to see what was going to happen here in the Bugs Bunny's crazy castle.

00:03:34.280 --> 00:03:37.300
However, uh, you know, and Joe, I think you said something similar last week.

00:03:37.560 --> 00:03:47.100
I'm familiar with the, the series of crazy castle games because I had, uh, on Game Boy Bugs Bunny's crazy castle too, where I'm pretty sure they dropped the, the, but maybe they didn't.

00:03:47.440 --> 00:03:52.120
And so I know, like I knew what these, what this game was going into it, but I thought, Oh, well I played it on the Game Boy.

00:03:52.280 --> 00:03:55.440
That's like, you know, not as good on hardware as the NES is.

00:03:55.540 --> 00:04:00.840
So maybe there's some new stuff here, but it's mostly the same kind of game as any of the other crazy castle games.

00:04:00.880 --> 00:04:03.800
It's a whole, it's a whole line of games with usually different characters too.

00:04:03.880 --> 00:04:05.180
It's not just a Bugs Bunny.

00:04:05.360 --> 00:04:08.960
It's whatever, whoever they can license winds up getting a crazy castle game.

00:04:08.960 --> 00:04:14.460
These crazy castle games of course are puzzle platforming games where, uh, in this one you're playing as Bugs Bunny.

00:04:14.580 --> 00:04:21.980
And the goal is to collect all of the carrots, uh, in each floor, uh, which is technically like a level, but you're climbing up the castle.

00:04:22.180 --> 00:04:24.080
So you're on like floor one, then floor two.

00:04:24.460 --> 00:04:26.660
Once you collect all the carrots though, you move on to the next stage.

00:04:26.800 --> 00:04:30.120
There's no keys, no hidden doors or anything like that, but there are enemies.

00:04:30.500 --> 00:04:36.100
Uh, Sylvester, the cat, Daffy Duck, Yosemite Sam all stand in your way.

00:04:36.100 --> 00:04:38.580
And, uh, sometimes in different colors too.

00:04:38.900 --> 00:04:40.820
Uh, you thought Sylvester was bad in black.

00:04:40.980 --> 00:04:42.120
Wait till you see him in green.

00:04:42.140 --> 00:04:43.440
He's way more devious.

00:04:44.340 --> 00:04:49.780
I did find it odd that like what they, they kept saying that they had all these characters and I did see them,

00:04:49.860 --> 00:04:53.960
but it seemed that most of them were just green and orange Sylvester.

00:04:54.240 --> 00:04:55.820
Um, I'm not sure what that was about.

00:04:55.940 --> 00:04:59.360
I saw a lot of Yosemite Sams and a couple of, uh, Wile E.

00:04:59.400 --> 00:05:02.720
Coyotes. Yes, there is Wild Coyote as well.

00:05:02.840 --> 00:05:03.680
I forgot to mention him.

00:05:03.680 --> 00:05:07.760
And there are some episodes, Wild Coyote doesn't just go up against Roadrunner.

00:05:07.880 --> 00:05:10.760
There are some episodes where he goes up against, uh, uh, Bugs.

00:05:11.340 --> 00:05:14.840
Daffy obviously always in, like anti-hero to Bugs, but.

00:05:15.160 --> 00:05:17.000
Well, that's when we're starting to go more MCU.

00:05:17.840 --> 00:05:20.280
Yeah, yeah. But he, but he does go up against, wait, I'm sorry.

00:05:20.360 --> 00:05:22.140
Did you say that he doesn't go up against Bugs Bunny?

00:05:22.240 --> 00:05:23.420
No, he does. Okay.

00:05:23.620 --> 00:05:25.580
Yeah, yeah. I'm saying often he did.

00:05:25.580 --> 00:05:30.300
Uh, it's not, he's not just a Roadrunner guy, but, you know, Sylvester, the cat?

00:05:30.560 --> 00:05:33.120
I don't think so. Let alone three different versions of him.

00:05:33.440 --> 00:05:35.320
Where is Elmer Fudd in this game?

00:05:35.420 --> 00:05:37.940
Like that is Bugs Bunny's number one enemy, right?

00:05:38.000 --> 00:05:39.800
Is Elmer Fudd and he's not even in the game.

00:05:40.420 --> 00:05:42.020
Yeah. Yeah, I didn't even think of that.

00:05:42.660 --> 00:05:45.160
Um. There's just a lack of roster altogether though, right, Joe?

00:05:45.280 --> 00:05:49.900
Like if you're thinking of Looney Tunes villains, I wouldn't even start with Daffy Duck.

00:05:50.500 --> 00:05:56.680
Yeah, well also, I mean I guess because for like, for like, recognizability Daffy Duck makes sense.

00:05:56.820 --> 00:05:57.800
But it's just, yeah, you're right.

00:05:57.880 --> 00:06:02.260
They have, there's such a huge roster of Looney Tunes characters to draw from.

00:06:02.700 --> 00:06:10.640
And it's weird that like they, they, they went to Green Sylvester before they went to a new character.

00:06:10.940 --> 00:06:15.960
Because another thing I was going to ask, you mentioned like, you mentioned the Green Sylvester and how he's coming to get you.

00:06:15.960 --> 00:06:26.100
Is that, now was that a serious comment where are you confirming my suspicion that the Green characters are more dangerous or have better AI than the other characters?

00:06:26.100 --> 00:06:27.920
Oh wow, I wouldn't even thought that.

00:06:28.400 --> 00:06:34.500
I thought that like, okay, that's how you know like all the Green, the Green enemies are harder than the regular enemies.

00:06:35.240 --> 00:06:42.480
Which I don't know if that's true or not, but if that's the case, I think it's silly to do that rather than just make each enemy have different behavior.

00:06:42.680 --> 00:06:44.800
You know, make Sylvester behave differently than Daffy.

00:06:44.800 --> 00:06:51.020
I want to say that it's not the case and that we're like sort of, yeah, we're projecting onto them.

00:06:51.700 --> 00:06:52.760
Overactive imagination.

00:06:53.120 --> 00:07:10.960
Yeah. But I want to say that my main theory as to why we only have this many licensed enemies is because I imagine maybe the company that owns the licenses, I don't remember, Warner Brothers, is it?

00:07:11.080 --> 00:07:12.340
I don't know. Yep, Warner Brothers.

00:07:12.340 --> 00:07:14.680
They're probably doing that like a la carte.

00:07:15.920 --> 00:07:21.220
So each specific enemy that you want, they probably had to pay more money to get.

00:07:21.420 --> 00:07:29.640
But would I be wrong to just say like Bugs Bunny can support his own video game where you don't need other recognizable characters then at that point?

00:07:29.640 --> 00:07:39.840
Like if you're going to just like why not just put him in like a new part of the Looney Tunes world where like it's a whole new land that he's unfamiliar with and he inherited this castle.

00:07:40.060 --> 00:07:43.180
Only if he can make it through all 60 floors or something like that.

00:07:43.280 --> 00:07:48.140
And they're unfamiliar enemies rather than like a bunch of Sylvester the cats walking around.

00:07:48.140 --> 00:07:49.300
Like that's an IP nightmare.

00:07:50.340 --> 00:08:00.000
I don't disagree that that's a weird – it was a weird decision for them to have several of the same character and rather than going to a bunch of different characters.

00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:13.220
But I feel like if they had done that and put him in like this new world where it was like Bugs Bunny faces all these new enemies, new stuff, we probably would be here like complaining about like it looks like they just took another game and slapped Bugs Bunny onto it.

00:08:13.220 --> 00:08:16.680
Whereas like at least this way, yeah, it's got other stuff.

00:08:16.780 --> 00:08:25.660
I'm not going to be satisfied with this game regardless because being Bugs Bunny is like one thing and how many characters from Looney Tunes is another thing.

00:08:25.820 --> 00:08:37.880
But Looney Tunes is a very slapstick style of comedy that this game doesn't bring outside of like some boxes falling on people's heads.

00:08:37.980 --> 00:08:39.820
It's not a very cartoonish game.

00:08:39.820 --> 00:08:47.820
There should have been more pranks or props implemented like you pick up a boxing glove and then you shoot it as like a projectile.

00:08:49.020 --> 00:08:50.600
These are all ideas, right?

00:08:50.640 --> 00:08:53.540
They're starting points, but that's all the game ever goes, right?

00:08:53.660 --> 00:08:57.680
It's like they got a 10-ton weight and you drop it from a platform and it hits somebody in the head.

00:08:57.820 --> 00:08:58.920
Those are funny things.

00:08:58.920 --> 00:09:10.440
But even the world itself, like the crazy castle, is mostly just like, you know, these empty bare rooms that have like no fun settings to them or anything like that.

00:09:10.540 --> 00:09:11.900
They don't feel familiar.

00:09:12.080 --> 00:09:26.360
There's so much to pull from here for Looney Tunes that I'm a little surprised that they didn't, you know, up the ante more and make the objective of killing your enemies or finishing levels be more puzzle-based around silly pranks.

00:09:26.360 --> 00:09:27.340
I see that too.

00:09:27.460 --> 00:09:35.760
I feel like I don't want to say that the game is like very cerebral because it's not like the puzzles get like super, super difficult or you have to think super hard about them or anything.

00:09:36.040 --> 00:09:42.900
But it's like the fact that you can't, the fact that it's not a game where you jump or where it's where you really just think about like where do I go next?

00:09:42.960 --> 00:09:44.000
How do I get from here to here?

00:09:44.260 --> 00:09:45.580
You're right. It doesn't feel cartoony.

00:09:45.640 --> 00:09:49.480
It doesn't feel, it doesn't feel bouncy and it doesn't feel like, I don't know.

00:09:49.480 --> 00:09:50.680
It doesn't feel silly and fun.

00:09:52.360 --> 00:09:58.480
And I'm using fun in, you know, as like a term to be like actiony, exciting fun.

00:09:58.580 --> 00:10:02.060
I'm not necessarily saying that there's nothing enjoyable or fun about this game.

00:10:02.440 --> 00:10:11.900
But yeah, it doesn't immediately scream like, it doesn't give me any of the same feelings I would get if I was watching a Looney Tunes cartoon where everything is colorful and bright and happening really fast and coming at you.

00:10:12.860 --> 00:10:17.940
Well, this game is, you said that it's more than just a Bugs Bunny thing.

00:10:17.940 --> 00:10:19.800
Is this the first of the Crazy Castles?

00:10:20.200 --> 00:10:22.400
This is the first of the Crazy Castles.

00:10:22.540 --> 00:10:30.280
Although I think even this one has some problems where like in Japan, it's actually Mickey Mouse and then here it's Bugs Bunny.

00:10:30.520 --> 00:10:36.440
And then in Europe, sometimes it's Ghostbusters or sometimes it's, I know this is crazy.

00:10:36.440 --> 00:10:38.380
We did this on the Mickey Mouse episode a long time ago.

00:10:38.520 --> 00:10:41.000
We broke down like which one for each game.

00:10:41.000 --> 00:10:44.000
But yeah, this is the very first entry in it.

00:10:44.160 --> 00:10:55.040
Well, that sounds like it was never going to take any actual like thematic input on whatever IP they're using because it's just a, it's just a reskin.

00:10:55.280 --> 00:11:07.180
Like it's never going to feel like Looney Tunes just because it's got carrots and a couple enemies that look like the characters in the show because it didn't matter.

00:11:08.220 --> 00:11:18.820
Yeah. This might be one of the earliest instances of just using a popular franchise from outside of a video game, putting it in a video game and just using that to sell your game.

00:11:18.940 --> 00:11:23.660
Because like, yeah, you recognize Bugs Bunny and Sylvester and Yosemite Sam and stuff.

00:11:23.660 --> 00:11:27.760
But like otherwise, none of this feels like a Looney Tunes game or anything.

00:11:28.300 --> 00:11:38.160
Yeah. Ironically, it sounds like this actually is what I said we'd be complaining about if it was Bugs Bunny in a new world where, yeah, they just slapped Bugs Bunny into an existing video game.

00:11:41.120 --> 00:11:49.220
So let's go, let's rewind a little bit and let's go back to just the idea of the game, which is collecting these carrots, you know, from floor to floor.

00:11:49.720 --> 00:11:55.040
The platforming in this game, it's interesting to call this a platforming game because there is no jumping.

00:11:55.160 --> 00:11:56.160
There is no ability to jump.

00:11:56.300 --> 00:12:02.660
Bugs Bunny can only walk and collect power-ups that he can, you know, like store temporarily.

00:12:02.960 --> 00:12:04.560
Yeah, I wouldn't call it a platforming game.

00:12:05.080 --> 00:12:16.920
Yeah, but I think you could argue that, you know, a lot of this game is platforming because it's about how you fall onto the other levels or what, you know, where you are in relation to the enemies, like using that to your advantage.

00:12:17.320 --> 00:12:25.600
There is, there's the puzzles in this game are understanding like the layout of the particular level so that you avoid the enemies appropriately.

00:12:25.800 --> 00:12:28.920
Because like, yeah, the first level is not going to give you a tough time.

00:12:28.920 --> 00:12:38.940
But later on, the enemies are kind of placed in a way where it's like, it causes very frenetic gameplay where the reactions have to be very like on point.

00:12:39.460 --> 00:12:55.540
And I think that's where like this platforming element comes in because if you're on, if you trap yourself on a particular like floor or something and don't have a door available to you or a place to fall to to get to the next platform, that's, that's how you lose.

00:12:55.540 --> 00:12:58.040
That is the, that is the core of the game here.

00:12:58.160 --> 00:13:04.940
That is the puzzle that you're trying to solve because you don't get the ability to take out every single enemy on every single stage.

00:13:05.100 --> 00:13:20.600
Sometimes they only give you like one boxing glove for three enemies and you might be quick to use it on the enemy right in front of you and then figure out that the actual puzzle was is that there's a guy later on who like, if you're not being really smart about where you're placing yourself, you might just want to use it on him.

00:13:20.600 --> 00:13:23.100
Sometimes this way you can collect the carrot behind him or something like that.

00:13:23.280 --> 00:13:27.120
Well, I wouldn't say that that means that it's a, it's definitely a puzzle game for sure.

00:13:27.260 --> 00:13:39.320
But I think it's more, you're not, you're not platforming so much as it's like a maze with, with enemies walking around and you can't like, it's got gravity.

00:13:39.320 --> 00:13:46.960
Like I did, I think in terms of, for it to be platforming, I think it would need to be more action based and not like decision based.

00:13:49.260 --> 00:13:50.180
So I don't know.

00:13:50.300 --> 00:13:51.540
That's just how I see it.

00:13:51.740 --> 00:13:57.460
Yeah. I feel like I'd have to see like, like the actual definition of a platforming game before I can really weigh in.

00:13:57.460 --> 00:14:11.500
But yeah, cause my gut is also just like, if it's not, if it's not jumping, I don't immediately see it as a platforming game, but, but I don't know if that's actually what makes it, I don't know if that actually is necessary for it to be a platformer.

00:14:12.280 --> 00:14:17.700
Well, other than the semantics of, of what the platforming is, what about the concept of these puzzles?

00:14:17.840 --> 00:14:21.920
The, the, you know, the items, the designs, the enemies throughout the, the core idea of the game.

00:14:22.020 --> 00:14:23.200
What do you guys think about that stuff?

00:14:23.860 --> 00:14:25.980
I think the concept is, is cool.

00:14:25.980 --> 00:14:34.860
I think it's a, you know, it's pretty basic, but it's, you know, just, it's just collect everything, collect everything on the screen without getting caught.

00:14:35.000 --> 00:14:46.500
You gotta, like you said, you gotta find your way around and really think about where the enemies are and, and conserve your weapons for when, when you absolutely have to take out an enemy or, or, you know, you have to be looking ahead a lot.

00:14:46.620 --> 00:14:48.560
And I think that's a, that's a cool idea for a puzzle.

00:14:48.700 --> 00:14:52.200
You can make some very interesting puzzles, um, out of that.

00:14:54.040 --> 00:14:55.180
I, I don't know.

00:14:55.980 --> 00:15:05.900
If in like the first, I think like 25 levels that I, that I did, if I necessarily say that they got super interesting or really particularly complicated.

00:15:06.140 --> 00:15:09.660
Um, sometimes they get kind of, sometimes you have to think a little differently there.

00:15:09.780 --> 00:15:14.800
They, they, they add like tubes and they add stairs that they have to think about like how to get to this level.

00:15:14.940 --> 00:15:16.920
And will this tube, let me skip that level.

00:15:18.740 --> 00:15:21.780
Um, so I, I guess overall it's like, it's an interesting concept.

00:15:22.840 --> 00:15:27.100
It never, it never got super, super difficult.

00:15:27.240 --> 00:15:31.880
It really made me like feel like I was solving a complex puzzle at any point.

00:15:32.000 --> 00:15:34.820
But again, I, I don't know how many levels there are in this game.

00:15:34.960 --> 00:15:39.960
I did between 20 and 25 of them, which maybe isn't enough for me to make that judgment.

00:15:39.960 --> 00:15:41.800
So I don't know what you guys thought about that.

00:15:42.320 --> 00:15:49.900
Yeah. I mean, I think that the, the basic gameplay loop is, um, it's entertaining enough to like,

00:15:49.900 --> 00:15:55.560
I guess if I had this on my phone, I would play a few rounds on my commute or something.

00:15:55.880 --> 00:16:02.520
Um, but I was also surprised that like, as long as I played it, there wasn't any real jump in difficulty.

00:16:02.520 --> 00:16:06.840
Like they, they experimented a little bit with like concepts for layouts.

00:16:06.840 --> 00:16:12.420
Like there's, um, yeah, we've got the tubes, we've got the doors, uh, with the, with the staircases.

00:16:12.940 --> 00:16:19.000
Um, uh, but they, um, it never really ups the ante really.

00:16:19.000 --> 00:16:24.580
So I, I can't really see it lasting for all that long of a play session.

00:16:24.800 --> 00:16:29.240
Yeah. When I think about like, sorry, I just was going to think about something like, like adventures of Lolo.

00:16:30.020 --> 00:16:32.920
Like that had levels in it where I had to like stop and think.

00:16:33.120 --> 00:16:35.820
And I had to, and I had to be like, okay, well, how can I work this out?

00:16:36.400 --> 00:16:39.800
This had always felt like I would maybe try to go through this tube and like, oh wait,

00:16:39.860 --> 00:16:40.940
that didn't take me where I need to go.

00:16:41.320 --> 00:16:43.340
Let me loop back around and just try the other tube.

00:16:43.540 --> 00:16:44.700
And like, okay, yep, that's the one.

00:16:45.060 --> 00:16:48.280
Like it always felt, it never felt like I needed to like really stop and be like, okay, wait a minute.

00:16:48.360 --> 00:16:52.860
What if I go here first and get this and like go there, this, like I never really needed that.

00:16:52.940 --> 00:16:55.540
I just kind of ran around avoiding people until I had all the carrots.

00:16:56.160 --> 00:16:59.760
Yeah. I think there's something about, you know, the repetitiveness of the whole game.

00:16:59.880 --> 00:17:05.280
Like the idea that it never evolves, but be any further than just collecting all the carrots in a particular stage.

00:17:05.420 --> 00:17:11.200
Just makes the later levels come down to, you know, timing, precision almost, right?

00:17:11.340 --> 00:17:13.200
It's not about solving puzzles.

00:17:13.360 --> 00:17:16.460
Like you said, Joe, there's no like stopping and thinking about where you're going.

00:17:16.600 --> 00:17:20.360
You have plenty of space to get to avoid enemies.

00:17:20.760 --> 00:17:24.360
You know, for the most part, I'd say some levels try to, the best levels even,

00:17:24.360 --> 00:17:25.860
actually make it a little cramped.

00:17:25.960 --> 00:17:31.700
So this way you do have to think about your particular enemy that you're dealing with at that moment and how you're going to get around him.

00:17:31.800 --> 00:17:33.280
I think that's the strongest part of the game.

00:17:33.440 --> 00:17:43.260
But for the most part, this is just a game that, you know, is almost like an action game in the sense that like your inputs really matter, you know,

00:17:43.260 --> 00:17:47.540
for what you're going to do next and it comes down to timing for a lot of these enemies.

00:17:48.680 --> 00:17:53.840
Because otherwise, the whole idea of just collecting the carrots is something that you could almost like stall for, right?

00:17:53.940 --> 00:18:04.780
You can just drag out enemies to chase you around areas that you know that you've already collected carrots for and then fall through a platform and reclimb your way back up.

00:18:04.780 --> 00:18:13.440
Or my favorite thing, and probably the most cartoonish thing about this game, is trick them into going up or down the stairs while you're going in the opposite direction.

00:18:13.460 --> 00:18:21.700
The idea that you can both pass each other on the stairs and it doesn't kill you is honestly maybe the most Looney Tunes thing that happens in this game.

00:18:21.700 --> 00:18:31.780
But it has to be part of the actual strategy because a lot of times I feel like that was the saving grace of getting past somebody who otherwise just kind of mirrors your inputs.

00:18:32.020 --> 00:18:36.020
Like if you're on the right side of the screen, he'll walk towards the right side of the screen.

00:18:36.160 --> 00:18:39.480
And then if you start heading the other direction, he turns around and comes back to you.

00:18:39.680 --> 00:18:41.160
There's a lot of like that behavior.

00:18:42.040 --> 00:18:53.260
So, you know, in a game like Pac-Man where the ghosts all behave however they want to, you don't have that problem because you – I mean you don't have that ability because as Pac-Man you always have to be moving.

00:18:53.440 --> 00:18:56.000
And there are like, you know, narrow passages.

00:18:56.640 --> 00:19:06.260
Bugs Bunny's Crazy Castle doesn't really do that because it's such a large level space and there's only like ever three to four enemies on a particular stage.

00:19:06.260 --> 00:19:12.080
But you get the ability to just walk back a little bit and think about your moment-to-moment gameplay.

00:19:12.260 --> 00:19:16.880
And that's why I say that the best levels in this game really come down to just precision timing.

00:19:17.120 --> 00:19:20.840
And I don't know if that's like necessarily a good thing for a puzzle game.

00:19:20.980 --> 00:19:22.480
Is it really precision though?

00:19:22.600 --> 00:19:25.640
Because like you said, like there's no timer for this game.

00:19:25.640 --> 00:19:30.560
And it seems like this is a game more so than others designed for children.

00:19:31.400 --> 00:19:34.860
I think the timer would make it a little bit harder.

00:19:34.860 --> 00:19:38.620
So I don't know if you really need precision because you can just wait it out.

00:19:39.060 --> 00:19:46.040
Well, if you – I think what I meant was is like if you look at the later levels, right, the ones that I was talking about like where they're a little more closed in.

00:19:46.520 --> 00:19:54.620
I was referring to those being the better levels because in those levels you actually have to think about how you're dealing with the enemy on your floor.

00:19:54.620 --> 00:20:09.600
So when you are closed in and you have to be precise with your movements because if you do the wrong – if you go the wrong way, if you can't trick an enemy, if you don't have the item anymore, you're boxed into a corner where they will catch you similar to Pac-Man.

00:20:09.600 --> 00:20:17.880
Where like you try to trick out one ghost but then there's another ghost in the other direction and now you have that like inevitable feeling of they're going to close in on you from either direction.

00:20:17.880 --> 00:20:18.820
You've trapped yourself.

00:20:19.140 --> 00:20:25.540
That's what happens in these later levels of the Bugs Bunny Crazy Castle that make the game – that's where it shines the best.

00:20:25.540 --> 00:20:27.780
But still, it's not about solving any puzzles.

00:20:27.920 --> 00:20:30.280
It's about being fast with your reflexes.

00:20:31.320 --> 00:20:40.320
Okay. Yeah, I ran into a couple towards the end of my playthrough where I would get trapped or where like I made this wrong decision and I got trapped.

00:20:40.500 --> 00:20:45.240
I did also notice though that that didn't happen to me as much as it should have.

00:20:45.660 --> 00:20:54.580
Especially like earlier on – it's almost like the AI is not quite like – I don't know, robust enough to really like –

00:20:56.200 --> 00:21:04.900
challenge the player because there were a lot of times where I was like, oh crap, like I went here, this guy's walking this way, now I see like I've trapped myself.

00:21:05.160 --> 00:21:10.760
But then like the guy would just turn around and like not trap me in the very obvious way he could have trapped me.

00:21:11.080 --> 00:21:11.960
I noticed that too, yeah.

00:21:11.960 --> 00:21:15.240
And that happened to be like quite a few times before finally I like would start to get trapped.

00:21:15.800 --> 00:21:22.040
And I only ever got trapped by the green Sylvester, which is what made me think like, does this guy have better AI than the other guys?

00:21:22.260 --> 00:21:23.400
Might have just been a coincidence.

00:21:23.400 --> 00:21:28.200
But all the other times it was always just like, oh crap, like they totally have me cornered.

00:21:28.460 --> 00:21:29.880
Like sometimes it was more than just obvious.

00:21:30.040 --> 00:21:34.880
Sometimes it was like their only option to not corner me was to just turn and walk away from me now.

00:21:35.060 --> 00:21:36.740
And then they would just turn and walk away from me.

00:21:37.040 --> 00:21:43.340
So I don't know, I felt like it was a little – either it was trying to go easy on the player or it was just not designed perfectly.

00:21:43.660 --> 00:21:47.760
Yeah, and I think that's what I'm getting at too, Joe, is that this game more than anything,

00:21:47.760 --> 00:21:53.940
you know, my closest parallel to it has been Pac-Man because that's ideally like that's the same game, right?

00:21:54.020 --> 00:21:57.960
Like the ghosts are chasing you and you're powerless to them unless you have the power pellet.

00:21:58.200 --> 00:22:03.440
In this game, it takes a similar approach where like these enemies that you otherwise are just completely,

00:22:04.160 --> 00:22:06.440
you know, like you're indefensible to them.

00:22:07.600 --> 00:22:12.360
Now all of a sudden, because there's so much more space, there's so many different ways to go

00:22:12.360 --> 00:22:17.040
and the only penalty is just like, oh, now I got to climb back up those stairs again

00:22:17.040 --> 00:22:19.280
because I jumped off the platform to get away from that enemy.

00:22:19.860 --> 00:22:24.980
You know, that's not really a punishment and it just prolongs like the experience of playing the game

00:22:24.980 --> 00:22:26.180
and collecting all the carrots.

00:22:26.900 --> 00:22:34.460
I just think they should have done more to make this, you know, feel like something you actually have to solve.

00:22:34.600 --> 00:22:36.480
Like, okay, the carrot's there, but I don't know how to get it.

00:22:36.580 --> 00:22:38.260
That never happens in this game.

00:22:38.300 --> 00:22:41.200
You never see a carrot and say, well, I don't know how I'm going to get that

00:22:41.200 --> 00:22:45.620
because you see just from the mechanics of the doors, the pipes, the stairs,

00:22:45.860 --> 00:22:49.000
you see where everything leads to right on its very own screen.

00:22:49.580 --> 00:22:55.580
Maybe some of the pipes try to trick you by being long enough that they're spread out across two screens,

00:22:55.580 --> 00:23:00.100
but all that takes is a simple walk over so that the screen scrolls a little bit

00:23:00.100 --> 00:23:02.240
and you'll see where the pipe ends up.

00:23:02.580 --> 00:23:07.340
So there's no real challenge to figuring out, like, you know,

00:23:07.400 --> 00:23:11.080
how do I collect the only thing I need to collect in this game?

00:23:11.280 --> 00:23:18.200
Yeah, it feels like each of the levels was sort of, like, designed to function and to work well,

00:23:18.300 --> 00:23:20.980
but not necessarily be a puzzle, you know?

00:23:21.160 --> 00:23:25.120
It was never like, how do I get the player to, you know, trick the player into going this way

00:23:25.120 --> 00:23:29.620
and then turns out that's the wrong way or hide the way that,

00:23:29.780 --> 00:23:34.120
hide the correct path from the player's, like, obvious view.

00:23:34.340 --> 00:23:37.060
So it doesn't sound like a very crazy castle to me.

00:23:37.820 --> 00:23:41.300
Maybe it's crazy at how a little thought was put into the puzzle.

00:23:43.460 --> 00:23:44.440
I love that.

00:23:50.160 --> 00:23:53.700
So there's 60 levels in the game and, you know, I didn't do it.

00:23:53.820 --> 00:23:59.500
I didn't play them all, but I did go to the cheat codes and because it's passwords.

00:23:59.840 --> 00:24:03.100
So I did look at, like, okay, well, what is the password for the 60th level?

00:24:03.260 --> 00:24:04.180
And I did that one.

00:24:04.180 --> 00:24:11.960
And, like, yeah, it was challenging, but I agree with Sean's, you know, idea that this is probably just a game designed for children

00:24:11.960 --> 00:24:17.100
who are familiar with VIP, be it Bugs Bunny or Mickey Mouse or Ghostbusters, right?

00:24:17.240 --> 00:24:19.300
It is a very basic game.

00:24:19.300 --> 00:24:20.400
It is a very simple game.

00:24:20.400 --> 00:24:26.060
And, you know, for some people, this might be enough of a game, right?

00:24:26.200 --> 00:24:29.580
Like, it doesn't actually, like, control too poorly.

00:24:29.660 --> 00:24:35.220
And the concept is laid out to you right from the very beginning, but it never, like, evolves.

00:24:35.380 --> 00:24:38.560
And I think for me, I'm struggling with that part.

00:24:38.560 --> 00:24:39.600
I don't like that.

00:24:39.740 --> 00:24:41.560
But maybe that is enough.

00:24:41.840 --> 00:24:48.160
Like, maybe just the idea of, like, a game that plays well enough and has a core objective

00:24:48.160 --> 00:24:55.460
and you just keep doing that over and over again, yeah, it feels like a very, you know, 10 years earlier arcade-style game.

00:24:55.760 --> 00:25:02.740
But it's a completely functional game that does exactly what, you know, what a Crazy Castle game, I guess, is supposed to do.

00:25:03.460 --> 00:25:10.380
I just feel like they should have put some more thought into the IP and other things that they clearly were never going to do.

00:25:10.480 --> 00:25:14.580
So we're just talking about this, you know, now in present day.

00:25:14.580 --> 00:25:20.060
Obviously, like, you know, this IP is just tied to whatever they could get the license for.

00:25:20.200 --> 00:25:24.760
Maybe in Japan they were able to get the license for Mickey Mouse, but, you know, you got to pay a fortune in America.

00:25:24.760 --> 00:25:26.280
So they were like, all right, we'll get Bugs Bunny.

00:25:26.360 --> 00:25:30.960
He's like Mickey Mouse. I think it was actually Roger Rabbit too at one point, which is interesting.

00:25:31.700 --> 00:25:35.800
But I don't really know where I'm going with this other than to say, like, there's not a whole lot to talk about here

00:25:35.800 --> 00:25:42.020
because you could play level 1 and play level 60 in the same playthrough, like, you know, as an experienced gamer,

00:25:42.140 --> 00:25:44.220
like someone who just knows video games and knows layouts.

00:25:44.540 --> 00:25:49.760
Like, you could play both and probably solve both of them in a few attempts.

00:25:50.340 --> 00:25:54.540
I'm not saying you're going to just, like, load up the game, type in the password for level 60 and solve it on the first try,

00:25:54.540 --> 00:25:59.860
but you're also not going to, like, be like, oh, well, I need to understand the way that the game works

00:25:59.860 --> 00:26:03.040
and, like, all the puzzles and the intricacies in order to beat level 60.

00:26:03.040 --> 00:26:11.900
I think almost everybody who listens to this podcast could beat level 60 in, like, you know, by their 10th attempt, hands down.

00:26:12.240 --> 00:26:17.400
And maybe this is like Wrecking Crew where it's not, you know, it's just like the levels are just the levels

00:26:17.400 --> 00:26:20.000
and you're just going for a high score, but it's not.

00:26:20.120 --> 00:26:25.320
It is a story objective of beating the game to rescue Honey Bunny,

00:26:25.360 --> 00:26:28.540
and you do get that ending once you get to the 60th,

00:26:28.600 --> 00:26:32.380
and there is no select stage thing like there was for Wrecking Crew.

00:26:32.380 --> 00:26:37.220
So there should have been at least some kind of evolving factor.

00:26:37.300 --> 00:26:40.620
Yeah, it's a puzzle game without any aha moments.

00:26:42.120 --> 00:26:45.460
It's, I know you said that the controls aren't terrible.

00:26:45.600 --> 00:26:52.720
They're not terrible, but they are very strange in that, like, up and right and down and left,

00:26:52.720 --> 00:26:58.940
or depending on, like, the direction you're facing, like, up and down can move forward,

00:26:58.940 --> 00:27:05.760
and you can't approach stairs from a certain angle.

00:27:05.760 --> 00:27:08.580
You have to, like, give it some space and then back it up.

00:27:08.580 --> 00:27:13.200
Like, it also isn't perfectly constructed is what I'm trying to say.

00:27:13.520 --> 00:27:22.460
So there's just a lot of, like, head-scratching moments of, like, I was wondering,

00:27:22.460 --> 00:27:24.260
who is this game designed for?

00:27:24.360 --> 00:27:29.440
And the only answer that makes any sense is that more so than most NES games,

00:27:29.440 --> 00:27:37.300
this is just a kid's game that they don't really expect you to have to think too hard about.

00:27:37.680 --> 00:27:40.340
Right. I was thinking that, like, the whole time I was playing it, too,

00:27:40.400 --> 00:27:43.180
because, yeah, it's never too complicated.

00:27:44.080 --> 00:27:47.600
But also, like, I just remember playing this as a kid on the Game Boy,

00:27:48.700 --> 00:27:50.260
and I loved it as a kid.

00:27:50.620 --> 00:27:54.460
And also, like, I wasn't good at it as a kid.

00:27:54.840 --> 00:27:59.560
So, like, I feel like I always remember, like, getting excited to play this, you know,

00:27:59.740 --> 00:28:02.320
on, like, a long car ride, and I'd bring all my Game Boy games,

00:28:02.400 --> 00:28:03.840
and I would never get very far.

00:28:05.120 --> 00:28:08.640
And I think that that's, like, I think that, you know, now when I play it,

00:28:08.640 --> 00:28:09.700
I'm like, well, this is way too easy.

00:28:11.320 --> 00:28:15.620
But, yeah, I mean, I think that they're still thinking of it as, like, okay, this is the...

00:28:16.580 --> 00:28:21.020
I know that it was, like, this isn't the order in which they decided things,

00:28:21.060 --> 00:28:25.180
but if they were, like, thinking of it as, like, this is the Looney Tunes audience,

00:28:25.180 --> 00:28:29.680
it's a kid's audience, even though, obviously, the game was made before the Looney Tunes property

00:28:29.680 --> 00:28:33.580
was added to it, but it seems like they're always putting a children's property,

00:28:34.660 --> 00:28:37.620
slapping a children's property onto this, so maybe that was always the case.

00:28:37.720 --> 00:28:38.620
Like, this should be for kids.

00:28:39.640 --> 00:28:41.480
You know, it's no Sesame Street 1, 2, 3.

00:28:43.020 --> 00:28:45.300
Yeah, Nostalgia Goggles on for a second,

00:28:45.300 --> 00:28:47.400
because I had the second one on Game Boy.

00:28:47.660 --> 00:28:53.200
I would say that I was not excited to, like, play the actual game

00:28:53.200 --> 00:28:55.640
and, you know, do the whole, like, collecting.

00:28:55.960 --> 00:28:57.760
In the second one, I think you collect keys,

00:28:57.760 --> 00:29:02.120
and you have to get enough keys to open up the final door or whatever in each level.

00:29:02.200 --> 00:29:04.700
I was just excited to play a Bugs Bunny game,

00:29:04.700 --> 00:29:06.780
so they did get me on the IP part.

00:29:07.000 --> 00:29:10.540
I don't think I was, like, excited for the actual, like, gameplay of it,

00:29:10.540 --> 00:29:12.580
but the IP was something I bought it on.

00:29:12.700 --> 00:29:16.840
So it's weird, because we definitely have a successful series here, right?

00:29:16.880 --> 00:29:19.300
They made, like, five of these Crazy Castle games,

00:29:19.340 --> 00:29:23.500
and for numerous different IPs, too, not just five total then.

00:29:23.500 --> 00:29:24.780
So it actually comes out to, like, 20.

00:29:25.600 --> 00:29:31.540
But, you know, I don't know if that's how we need to look at it

00:29:31.540 --> 00:29:34.320
as people who look at games, like, based on, like,

00:29:34.380 --> 00:29:35.960
what you have available to you on PS5.

00:29:36.160 --> 00:29:38.080
Like, I think we can say, like, you know,

00:29:38.120 --> 00:29:39.900
Sean, you were talking about the controls not being too tight.

00:29:40.160 --> 00:29:44.160
I completely agree, too, that, like, they're serviceable,

00:29:44.160 --> 00:29:47.520
but there are weird moments in stiffness, too, with Bugs,

00:29:47.520 --> 00:29:51.400
where I feel like sometimes he takes, like, an extra step to the right

00:29:51.400 --> 00:29:54.660
or the way that the free fall works, it just, like,

00:29:54.720 --> 00:29:57.360
you're just hanging there, and then that causes, like, weird moments

00:29:57.360 --> 00:29:59.200
where you just know you're going to die.

00:29:59.780 --> 00:30:02.300
I guess a consequence of the system they set up anyway.

00:30:03.520 --> 00:30:08.160
But there's just a, overall, this game might be a children's game,

00:30:08.200 --> 00:30:11.940
but, like, you know, if we're not going to look at Sesame Street 1, 2, 3 that way,

00:30:12.020 --> 00:30:15.660
we shouldn't look at Bugs Bunny's Crazy Castle with that pass, either.

00:30:15.660 --> 00:30:19.340
Oh, yeah, never meant for it to, never intended for that to be a pass.

00:30:19.560 --> 00:30:22.000
I'm just squaring up my Essential Games list votes.

00:30:22.100 --> 00:30:25.080
You know, I don't want the kids' table to get a vote.

00:30:28.560 --> 00:30:33.860
One other thing that bothers me about this game is the password screen.

00:30:34.060 --> 00:30:36.100
It happens after every single level.

00:30:36.580 --> 00:30:40.400
You collect the final carrot or whatever, and you see Bugs Bunny jump for joy.

00:30:40.640 --> 00:30:43.200
Then you get the password screen, and it says,

00:30:43.300 --> 00:30:44.580
that's all, folks.

00:30:44.780 --> 00:30:48.580
Yeah. Clearly a Looney Tunes thing, you know, like a ha-ha-ha.

00:30:48.660 --> 00:30:50.880
They actually included, like, a good reference there.

00:30:51.020 --> 00:30:56.280
But it doesn't have the, like, you know, the circular record spinning look thing

00:30:56.280 --> 00:30:57.860
that, like, Looney Tunes is famous for.

00:30:57.980 --> 00:30:59.000
It just has a star.

00:30:59.880 --> 00:31:01.380
And that's all, folks, to me.

00:31:01.560 --> 00:31:02.560
Sounds like game over.

00:31:02.980 --> 00:31:06.580
Sounds like I ate too many carrots, got full, and now I'm done.

00:31:06.820 --> 00:31:08.960
Like, pick it up again tomorrow, Bugs.

00:31:09.540 --> 00:31:10.560
You're done for the day.

00:31:10.640 --> 00:31:15.440
That's all, folks. And they probably could have thought of something a little better and save That's All, folks,

00:31:15.640 --> 00:31:16.980
for a game over screen.

00:31:17.280 --> 00:31:18.920
Yeah. Which is also the same thing, though.

00:31:19.000 --> 00:31:20.140
The game over screen is the same.

00:31:21.660 --> 00:31:23.920
Yeah. Couldn't have been, like, a WhatsApp doc?

00:31:24.100 --> 00:31:25.240
Even though that doesn't really make sense either.

00:31:25.320 --> 00:31:26.240
Something makes funny sense.

00:31:26.240 --> 00:31:28.140
It's their only WhatsApp doc in the manual.

00:31:29.140 --> 00:31:30.660
Oh, well, yeah. I guess they only get one.

00:31:30.800 --> 00:31:32.500
I think that was a part of their licensing agreement.

00:31:32.640 --> 00:31:35.000
Yeah. Did it have a trademark next to it?

00:31:35.180 --> 00:31:36.340
I don't think so.

00:31:37.520 --> 00:31:40.220
But also, he's usually talking to Elmer Fudd, right?

00:31:40.820 --> 00:31:43.780
Yeah. Like, we're not doc, but I think most people are doc to him.

00:31:44.660 --> 00:31:46.420
Oh, okay. Yeah.

00:31:47.020 --> 00:31:49.480
Bugs would say WhatsApp doc to almost anybody.

00:31:49.480 --> 00:31:54.600
But you're right. In this universe, without Elmer Fudd, did it ever occur to him to say WhatsApp doc?

00:31:54.620 --> 00:31:57.000
In this alternate Bugs Bunny reality.

00:32:02.920 --> 00:32:06.380
On the sequels and spinoff side, I don't really know how to break this down.

00:32:06.780 --> 00:32:08.300
So we're just not going to.

00:32:08.640 --> 00:32:12.520
But, you know, Kemco is the developer here.

00:32:12.680 --> 00:32:14.520
Crazy Castle is their series.

00:32:15.720 --> 00:32:20.140
And there's, like, a bunch of these across different properties and IPs.

00:32:20.200 --> 00:32:21.200
And it gets so confusing.

00:32:21.520 --> 00:32:27.300
But the main idea to know is that if you played Crazy Castle games in North America,

